.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class BOUNDARY_COMPLEX> string \fBcsmp::BoundaryInterface\fP< dim, BOUNDARY_COMPLEX >::CreateBoundaryNameFrom (const \fBFaceConstructionData\fP< dim > & , const std::vector< std::string > & region_names) const\fR [protected]\fP"

.PP
combines strings (including 'BOUNDARY') into a unique name of the boundary LOCAL METHOD (not known beyond this compilation unit)

.PP
creates underscore-separated unique names for the region patches based on the juxtapositions relationships across the lower-dimensional regions, the names are composed of:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
the name of the master region
.IP "2." 4
"BOUNDARY"
.IP "3." 4
the patch identifier number attached to boundary
.IP "4." 4
the name of the inner region, i\&.e\&. the region that the lower-dimensional element normals point away from
.IP "5." 4
the name of the outer region, i\&.e\&. that into which the normals point
.PP

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
where the boundary just intersects a single layer (same material on either side), the layer name appears only once\&. The second instance is replaced by INTERSECTION\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class BOUNDARY_COMPLEX> pair< string, bool > \fBcsmp::BoundaryInterface\fP< dim, BOUNDARY_COMPLEX >::CreateExternalBoundaryFrom (const char * dimension_minus1_region, bool check_topo_attributes_of_nodes)"

.PP
converts lower-dimensional \fBRegion\fP on the outside of the model into a \fBBoundary\fP; returns whether this conversion was successful as well as the boundary name converts lower-dimensional \fBRegion\fP on the outside of the model into a \fBBoundary\fP; returns whether this conversion was successful as well as the boundary name 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class BOUNDARY_COMPLEX> pair< set< string >, bool > \fBcsmp::BoundaryInterface\fP< dim, BOUNDARY_COMPLEX >::CreateInternalBoundaryFrom (const char * dim_1_region)"

.PP
converts lower-dimensional \fBRegion\fP into uniquely named \fBBoundary\fP patches, assigning more specific names to these; the input \fBRegion\fP and its elements are removed Converts lower-dimensional region into Boundarie(s) of faces, decomposed into patches\&. The \fBMeshManager\fP updates the connectivity\&. The original region is removed (including removal from "Model") to non-unique regions\&.

.PP
Uses node-to-parent relationship to find the higher dimensional elements that will share a face with the \fBFace\fP: usng \fBhigherDimensionalNeighbors()\fP

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
pair of the set oof boundary patches that were created from the region and boolean that tells whether the operation was completely successful\&.
.RE
.PP
Algorithmic steps:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Verify input lower-dimensional region object from which the boundary shall be created: must be lower dimensional and must lie inside of model
.IP "2." 4
Determine the number of boundary segments (sub-boundaries) that the new boundary will consist of\&. The output of this step will be a map of \fBFaceConstructionData\fP in which the names of the new boundary segments are the keys\&.
.IP "3." 4
In the \fBMeshManager\fP object,

.PP
3\&.1 create all required face objects

.PP
3\&.2 connect them with one another (neighbors); Boundary::EstablishNeighborConnectivity( vector<Face<dim>*>& ); this is important because any \fBModelSubDomain\fP creation relies on this connectivity during identification of interior and perimeter\&.
.IP "4." 4
Create the \fBBoundary\fP segments, one-by-one from the map<bname, FaceConstructionData>; this involves connecting the faces to their higher dimensional neighbours\&.
.IP "5." 4
For the nodes located on the new boundaries, update / recreate the parent element vectors so that these no longer include neither the elements from which the \fBBoundary\fP was created nor the new faces\&.
.IP "6." 4
Remove the parent region of the new boundary(ies) from the region “Model\&.” The region is also put into the non-unique region map\&.
.IP "7." 4
Assign \fBBOX_BOUNDARY\fP flags to new nodes if any\&.
.IP "8." 4
By default, but optional removes parent region (including its elements)\&. 
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class BOUNDARY_COMPLEX> set< string > \fBcsmp::BoundaryInterface\fP< dim, BOUNDARY_COMPLEX >::EstablishBoundariesFromRegions ()"

.PP
inserts box boundary or irregular \fBcsmp::Boundary\fP objects for all eligible regions in the model; returns the names of the created boudaries Creates boundary around "Model" region determining whether lower-dimensional regions shall become a boundary segments on the basis of whether their name contains the string "BOUNDARY" or is one of the reserved boundary names\&.

.PP
When a boundary (of \fBFace\fP objects) is created, the underpinning lower-dimensional (parent) region is removed from the region "Model"\&. It is also moved from the unique to the non-unique region map if it was stored there originally\&.

.PP
\fBRemarks\fP
.RS 4
builds boundaries only considering unique, lower-dimensional regions (line-element boundaries will not be created)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
if all the created \fBFace\fP objects can be assigned to boundaries (as identified by the string BOUNDARY) the method returns true\&. If not, the remaining \fBFace\fP objects will be retained as a \fBBoundary\fP object named "Model_BOUNDARY and the method returns false\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
by contrast to \fBEstablishBoxBoundaries()\fP, EstablishBoundaries() does not require to be applied to box-shaped models, but is more general\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBTest\fP
.RS 4
updated by SKM 2016 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class BOUNDARY_COMPLEX> bool \fBcsmp::BoundaryInterface\fP< dim, BOUNDARY_COMPLEX >::EstablishBoxBoundaries ()"

.PP
creates \fBcsmp::Boundary\fP objects replacing lower-dimensional \fBBOX_BOUNDARY\fP named regions with boundaries with the same names Forms boundaries considering names of \fBBox\&.h\fP-defined strings only, e\&.g\&., BACK, BOTTOM, RIGHT, TOP, FRONT, IRREGULAR and INTERNAL\&. Regions with these names are converted to boundaries removing them and their Elements that are replaced by \fBFace\fP objects\&. The new boundaries are given the standard names and are flagged correspondingly\&.

.PP
\fBRemarks\fP
.RS 4
input regions must be lower-dimensional (surfaces in 3D and lines in 1D) 

.PP
input regions must be unique (space exclusive) 

.PP
connectivity of \fBFace\fP objects must match / preserve that of previous \fBElement\fP objects 

.PP
when elements become faces they need to be tagged so that one can detect which region they were created from (idx?)
.RE
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
if the TOP region is missing, but a IRREGULAR region is there in stead, this is converted into the corresponding boundary\&. In this case, the model is still regarded as BOX_SHAPED\&.

.PP
\fBBoundary\fP creation itself does not deal with the generation of \fBBOX_BOUNDARY\fP flags for the model edges and corners\&. This is accomplished subsequently (in this method) by calling \fBrecreateBoxBoundaryFlags()\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
this method does not take care of the updating of the non-unique regions that are affected by the conversion of Regions into boundaries\&. This is done afterwards by methods of the \fBModel\fP class\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
refactored by SKM 2016 

.PP
refactored by SKM 2018 

.PP
SKM 2021 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class BOUNDARY_COMPLEX> void \fBcsmp::BoundaryInterface\fP< dim, BOUNDARY_COMPLEX >::EstablishBoxBoundariesFromNodeFlags (bool recreate_box_boundary_flags_before)"

.PP
creates 3D BOX boundaries using the node flags to identify sides, edges, and corners; use for simple models where corresponding lines or surfaces are missing Creates BOX boundaries using the node flags to identify sides, edges, and corners; use for simple models where corresponding lines or surfaces are missing\&.

.PP
The result are \fBBoundary\fP objects with standard names such as TOP, BOTTOM, INTERNAL, IRREGULAR, EDGE1, CNR2 etc\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
method assumes that boundary flags were assigned correctly to the nodes, else a method which does this is called\&.
.RE
.PP
Procedure
.IP "\(bu" 2
using the node flags of the perimeter elements faces, boundary by boundary \fBVData\fP are created that are subsequently employed to create the \fBFace\fP objects of the new boundaries\&. Then these are created\&. 
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class BOUNDARY_COMPLEX> bool \fBcsmp::BoundaryInterface\fP< dim, BOUNDARY_COMPLEX >::EstablishBoxBoundariesFromOrientation ()"

.PP
tries to create \fBBox\fP \fBBoundary\fP objects surrounding '\fBModel\fP' into TOP, BOTTOM, IRREGULAR if possible; updates \fBBOX_BOUNDARY\fP flags Method caps elements that share one of their faces with the model boundary with \fBFace\fP objects\&. In a second step it assigns these to the \fBBox\fP boundaries: TOP, BOTTOM,LEFT, RIGHT, etc\&. INTERNAL, IRREGULAR, or \fBEdge\fP boundaries are not created\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
method assumes that the perimeter of region "Model" correctly identifies the outside faces of the elements of the model\&. 

.PP
this method was designed primarily for three-dimensional models\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
SKM 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
21/8/2018 
